<h2>The statutory basis for conditions</h2>
<p>Two provisions of the BNSS carry the weight. Section 480(3) (formerly Section 437(3) CrPC) empowers the Magistrate, when granting bail in offences of the kind POCSO involves, to impose conditions considered necessary — including conditions to ensure the person does not commit a similar offence and does not tamper with evidence or influence witnesses. Section 483 BNSS (formerly Section 439 CrPC) gives the Court of Session and the High Court their general bail jurisdiction, which carries with it a correspondingly wide power to craft conditions. In POCSO matters most contested bail applications are decided by the Sessions Court designated as the Special Court, or by the High Court, so Section 483 is where the conditions jurisprudence actually lives.</p>
<p>One structural point frames everything else: in POCSO proceedings the victim has a recognised right to be heard on bail, a subject this site covers in a separate explainer; the conditions discussed here are frequently shaped by what the child's side places before the court at that hearing.</p>

<h2>The POCSO-specific conditions</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Absolute no-contact</h4><p>No contact with the victim or the victim's family — direct, indirect or electronic. The condition covers calls, messages, social media, and approaches through relatives, friends or intermediaries. Indirect contact is the clause most often breached, usually through a well-meaning family member floating a compromise.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Distance and residence</h4><p>Staying away from the victim's locality, street or school; in same-neighbourhood or same-family cases, a direction to reside outside the area for the duration of the trial. The geography is specified in the order — a named colony, a radius, or a particular institution.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Reporting to the IO</h4><p>Marking presence before the investigating officer at stated intervals, typically tapering as the investigation concludes, so the condition tracks its purpose rather than outliving it.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Travel restraint</h4><p>Surrender of passport or a bar on leaving the country without leave, standard where there is any suggestion of flight or where the accused has overseas ties.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The balancing exercise</h2>
<p>The purpose of these conditions is twofold and specific: protecting the child from intimidation, inducement and re-traumatisation, and protecting the trial, in which the child is usually the central witness. Conditions calibrated to those purposes are legitimate however strict they feel. What conditions may not do is slide into punishment before verdict. A condition has crossed that line when it no longer serves the child or the trial but simply burdens the accused — an open-ended banishment from a city where the victim no longer lives, a reporting schedule that makes employment impossible, or terms so vague that the accused cannot know what compliance requires. The working test is performability with purpose: every condition should be capable of being obeyed, verifiable in its obedience, and traceable to the safety of the child or the integrity of the proceedings.</p>

<h2>Relocation conditions and their hardship</h2>
<p>The hardest cases are those where the accused and the child are neighbours or relatives — a substantial share of POCSO dockets. A no-contact order between people who share a staircase is an illusion, so courts reach for relocation: reside outside the locality until the child's evidence is recorded, or until the trial ends. The protective logic is sound, but the hardship is real and falls on more people than the accused — a displaced family, a lost tenancy, a job tied to the area. Two mitigations recur in practice. First, time-limiting the condition to the sensitive phase of the trial, typically until the child and the material witnesses have deposed. Second, defining the geography precisely, so that the accused can work in the city while staying out of the defined zone. An application that proposes such a calibrated condition is more persuasive than one that simply protests the hardship.</p>

<h2>When geography makes compliance unworkable</h2>
<p>Circumstances change: a workplace shifts, a family member falls ill inside the prohibited zone, the victim's family itself relocates. The route is an application for modification of conditions before the court that imposed them or the superior court — the mechanics are covered in this site's separate explainer on modifying bail conditions. The short point here: modification applications in POCSO matters succeed when they preserve the substance of the child's protection while adjusting its geography, and fail when they read as an attempt to re-litigate the conditions themselves.</p>

<h2>Breach and cancellation</h2>
<p>The enforcement mechanism behind every condition is cancellation of bail. An approach to the victim's family, presence in the prohibited locality, or any attempt to influence the child's testimony invites an application by the prosecution or the complainant for cancellation, and courts treat breaches involving child witnesses with severity. Two disciplines protect an accused on bail: strict literal compliance, including restraining relatives from any approach to the other side; and documentation — keeping a record of reporting dates and, where a condition is ambiguous, seeking clarification from the court rather than self-interpreting it.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Conditions are not boilerplate, and orders in POCSO cases are increasingly tailored to the actual proximity between the accused and the child. An accused preparing for a bail hearing should come with a concrete, performable set of proposed conditions; a victim's family attending the hearing should come with the specific facts — shared lanes, common schools, past approaches — that justify the tailored terms they seek.</p></div>
